  "teaId": "dorcas-needle",
  "text": "When she died, the widows didn't make speeches. They held up the clothes she made them.\n\nActs nine. In Joppa there's a disciple named Tabitha, which translated is Dorcas. And the text says she was full of good works and almsdeeds which she did.\n\nThen she gets sick, and she dies.\n\nThey wash her and lay her in an upper chamber. Lydda is near Joppa, and the disciples hear Peter is there, so they send two men and beg him to come without delay.\n\nPeter comes. They bring him upstairs.\n\nAnd here is the scene, and it wrecks me every time.\n\n\"All the widows stood by him weeping, and shewing the coats and garments which Dorcas made, while she was with them.\"\n\nThey are holding up the clothes. That's the eulogy. Not a speech. Evidence.\n\nAnd you have to understand what widows were in that world. Almost no safety net. And this woman had been quietly making them clothes with her own hands.\n\nPeter puts everybody out of the room, kneels down, prays, and turns to the body. \"Tabitha, arise.\"\n\nAnd she opened her eyes. And sat up.\n\nAnd he called the saints and widows and presented her alive.",
